{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a Crusader nobleman and prince of the 12th century. He was the son of Pons a former Count of Tripoli and his wife Cecilia of France a daughter of King Louis VI of France. Raymond was born around 1115 and spent much of his youth in the Kingdom of Jerusalem under the tutelage of his uncle Baldwin II who was the king at the time.\n\n### *highlighted section part 1*: Early Life and Background\nRaymond's upbringing was steeped in the political and military culture of the Crusader states he was well educated and learned the nuances of governance war and diplomacy. His mother Cecilia was a pivotal figure in his life she was known for her intelligence and influence which likely contributed to his own capabilities as a leader. The early years of Raymond’s life were marked by the instability common in the Crusader states with frequent conflicts and power struggles among the local nobles.\n\nBy the time Raymond came of age the Principality of Antioch was under the rule of his cousin Bohemond II. Raymond initially served under Bohemond in various military campaigns. However tensions arose between them partly due to Raymond’s ambition and partly due to Bohemond’s volatile personality. These tensions would later escalate into open conflict.\n\n### *highlighted section part 2*: Conflict with Bohemond II\nIn 1136 Raymond succeeded his father Pons as the Count of Tripoli. The same year he married Hodierne a daughter of Joscelin II Count of Edessa. Their union was strategic aiming to strengthen alliances between the Crusader states. However the marriage was short lived as Hodierne died soon after.\n\nThe conflict with Bohemond II reached its peak in 1137. Bohemond accused Raymond of plotting against him and demanded Raymond’s submission. When Raymond refused Bohemond marched an army toward Tripoli. Raymond prepared for war but was outmaneuvered by Bohemond’s forces. He sought help from King Fulk of Jerusalem but before reinforcements could arrive Bohemond’s army surrounded Tripoli. Facing certain defeat Raymond surrendered to Bohemond on the condition that his life and properties be spared.\n\nBohemond however was not satisfied with Raymond’s submission. He imprisoned Raymond and held him in Antioch for nearly a year. During this time Raymond’s wife Hodierne had died and he became engaged to Alice daughter of Baldwin II of Jerusalem. The engagement was part of a broader political deal to reconcile Raymond with the Jerusalem court.\n\n### *highlighted section part 3*: Later Career and Death\nRaymond was eventually released from captivity in 1138 and returned to Tripoli. He married Alice and the two worked to strengthen their domains. Raymond became a key player in the political landscape of the Crusader states often mediating disputes and forming alliances. He was known for his military prowess and diplomatic skills which helped stabilize the region to some extent.\n\nIn 1152 Raymond participated in the siege of Damascus alongside King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. The siege ended in failure and Raymond’s forces suffered heavy losses. This marked a turning point in his later years as his influence began to wane. By the 1160s Raymond was aging and his health was declining.\n\nRaymond III died in 1152 though some sources suggest his death occurred later around 1163. The exact date remains uncertain. His legacy lies in his contributions to the Crusader states his role in stabilizing Tripoli and his efforts to maintain peace among the fractured noble houses of the time. He left behind a daughter named Hodierna who married Raymond III of Tripoli’s cousin and eventual successor Raymond of Antioch.\n\nRaymond’s life reflects the turbulent nature of the Crusader states where alliances shifted rapidly and survival often depended on military strength and diplomatic cunning. His story is a testament to the complex interplay of power politics and personal ambition that characterized the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " also", "logprob": -1.730838656425476}, {"token": " known", "logprob": -0.04531657695770264}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.03303052857518196}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.12591156363487244}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.4887281060218811}, {"token": " Antio", "logprob": -0.21861356496810913}, {"token": "ch", "logprob": -3.909988299710676e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2954932153224945}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.2654343843460083}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -1.5045514106750488}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -0.00031537804170511663}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -1.2395145893096924}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.0543534941971302}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.6527925729751587}, {"token": " prince", "logprob": -1.6455726623535156}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.106656789779663}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.1723904013633728}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.4268392324447632}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00032240914879366755}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.002516557462513447}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -8.093983342405409e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -0.0021738011855632067}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.24604827165603638}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.35859107971191406}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.5193765163421631}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.6648833751678467}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -1.0970427989959717}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0020007132552564144}, {"token": " Pons", "logprob": -1.129700779914856}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -2.6510